// n23 — a fixed-point literal with more significant digits than a `fixed` has.
//
// CORBA 3.4 §7.11.3 caps a `fixed` at 31 significant digits; this one has 32.
// Split out of n22 on 2026-08-25: lexing reports one failure per file, so a
// second bad literal written beside the first is never reached and the claim
// that it was refused is a claim nothing checked.
//
// omniidl 4.3.4, measured 2026-08-25:
//   Fixed point constant has too many digits
//
// **We are stricter than the oracle, deliberately, and only on one side.**
// omniidl rejects 32 *integer* digits — as here — and silently truncates 32
// *fractional* digits to 31: `0.1234…9012d` dumps back as `0.1234…901d`,
// measured 2026-08-21. Silently dropping a digit from a constant is the exact
// failure that batch existed to close, so both are refused here and the
// divergence is recorded in corpus/divergences.tsv.
//
// Our refusal files under `fixed-literal`, not under the generic `parse` rule,
// which is not a detail: `orbweaver-forge` keys the fix hint for a malformed
// fixed-point literal to `fixed-literal`, and this exact shape spent a while
// filing under `parse` and never receiving it.
//
// 유효숫자는 31자리가 상한이다. 어휘 실패는 파일당 하나만 보고되므로 n22에서
// 분리했다.
module n23 {
  const fixed TOO_MANY = 12345678901234567890123456789012d;
};
